    "name": "bodh-brand-voice",
    "text": "---\nname: bodh-brand-voice\ndescription: How BODH (by Asttrolok) sounds in captions, scripts and replies.\n---\n\n# BODH brand voice\n\nBODH is a Vedic astrology (Jyotish) app from Asttrolok. People sign up, enter their birth details and get their kundli (birth chart), then pay for deeper readings and consultations.\n\n**Sound like** a knowledgeable friend who studied Jyotish properly: warm, calm, specific. Explain one idea at a time. Respect the tradition without sounding like a temple notice.\n\n**Do**\n- Lead with something the reader recognises in their own life (a Saturn return feeling, a Rahu-Ketu axis question, why their moon sign differs from their sun sign).\n- Use Sanskrit terms with a short plain-language gloss the first time: \"Shani (Saturn)\", \"dasha (planetary period)\".\n- Write in English by default. Hinglish is fine for Reels and Shorts hooks when it sounds natural (\"Aapka Shani kahan baitha hai?\"). Never mix scripts within a word.\n- Keep captions skimmable: a hook line, two to four short lines, then the call to action.\n- Calls to action point at the app: \"Get your free kundli on BODH\", \"See your dasha timeline in the app\". One CTA per post.\n\n**Don't**\n- Promise outcomes: no guaranteed marriage, job, money, cures or \"100% accurate\". No fear: no \"your life is in danger\", no curses, no doshas framed as doom.\n- Push remedies for money (gemstones, pujas) in organic posts.\n- Use clickbait like \"You won't believe\", \"shocking\", ALL CAPS words, or more than two emoji per caption.\n- Talk about other astrology apps by name.\n\n**Hashtags**: three to six, mixing the brand kit defaults with one or two topical tags. LinkedIn: at most three.\n"
